WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  rivian lucid lordstown canoe and fiskar  five ev startups i profiled a year ago  that all elicited high valuations under  the promise of creating electric  vehicles that could take on tesla's  dominance but fast forward to today and  the share price of those companies has  plummeted part of the reason well some  of these newcomers simply haven't made  as many cars as they'd hoped and all of  them are yet to turn a profit operating  a plant that's not producing a lot of  cars i mean there's no better way to run  through cash and now some are hitting a  make or break moment so where do these  companies stand and what are they doing  to survive let's find out  firstly if you want a snapshot of where  these companies were a year ago then go  check out the profile i made on them  back in 2021. back then none of these  companies had actually produced a  commercial vehicle in the auto industry  nothing is harder than like trying to  move from a prototype to mass production  that's wsj's detroit bureau chief  christina rogers who told me that while  trying to make a new car can be  challenging for any automaker trying to  do so at a time of supply chain woes  covered outbreaks chip shortages  geopolitical unrest the tech sell-off  and inflation can make it even harder  it's hard for even the traditional  automakers for smaller companies with  unproven track records it's even harder  for them to get suppliers to trust them  or to put them at the top of the list in  a recent letter to investors rivian said  that its card launchers have been  executed during one of the most complex  operating environments in decades  lordstown says like many others in the  industry it has suffered from part  shortages and raw material availability  canoe ceo told me that on any given day  the company is wrestling with supply for  around 60 parts though it's a fraction  of the 1800 that go into making the  vehicle rivien said semiconductor  shortages had led to delays and lucid  has said that difficulty getting hold of  carpet and glass had hampered its  production efforts visco meanwhile said  it was working with manufacturers magnet  and foxconn to proactively address any  supply chain challenges  so how many cars have these companies  actually made or delivered because there  is a difference and what do delays mean  for them well rivien slashed its 2022  production forecast from 50 000 to 25  000 vehicles so far the company says  it's manufactured around 5000 units  since it started production in 2021.  luxury sedan maker lucid also cut its  forecast from 20 000 to between 12 and  14 000 vehicles  so far it says it's delivered 360. and  while canoe said it hopes to produce  between 3 000 to 6 000 vehicles in 2022  so far it's only made 39 of its gamma  testing models and is currently  producing around eight vehicles a week  lordstown which had hoped to be  producing vehicles in september 2021  pushed back production to the third  quarter of 2022 and has said it needs to  raise an additional 150 million dollars  in capital before the end of the year  fiska however says it remains on track  to produce its first vehicles this year  not producing many or any vehicles means  one thing none of these companies is  turning a profit they're carrying heavy  losses in the first quarter of 2022  these companies posted millions of  dollars in losses and while rivien and  lucid are both sat on sizable  investments both canoe and lordstown  have at separate times said there is a  substantial doubt about the company's  ability to continue when i asked about  their losses lucid and fiskar declined  to comment lordstown referred me to  their earnings and rivien referred me to  a previous wsj interview canoe however  told me their strategy was to raise  capital whenever they hit major  milestones so what can these companies  do to try and survive well one method is  to simplify configuration options to get  more vehicles off the production line in  an investor call rivian said it was  doing just that prioritizing certain  trim options to maximize the number of  vehicles it can produce another method  is to simply raise the price of your  vehicle both rivian and lucid have done  just that raising the price for  customers who haven't pre-ordered before  a certain date and fiska 2 has said it  can't commit to pricing after the first  40 000 units all vehicles produced from  2024 onwards others have sold off assets  to generate more cash lordstown recently  sold off its ohio factory to foxconn you  will manufacture lordstown's vehicle for  a purchase price of 230 million dollars  and where you make your vehicle can make  a difference canoe told me they'd move  manufacturing from europe to the us to  be less exposed to currency swings and  supply chain snarls  so will these companies make it well  christina said that changes in  production or delivery rates was the  simplest way to tell whether these  companies were overcoming their  production was but if 2022 has taught us  one thing it's that supply chains are  fragile so even if production rates  increase it might be worth keeping an  eye on how much cash these companies  have left honestly the evs that have the  most cash or have easy access to cash  are probably the ones that are going to  prevail this business is very simple  cost a lot of money to build a car and  get a car off the ground  and if you don't have it that's it  game over  [Music]

